 ## Overview
-Surrounding Path Contribution approximates the complete path integral by taking into account only the contribution of "surrounding" paths [S1]. It exploits the fact that the action function has a global minimum at the Most Probable Path (MPP), allowing the integrand to be expanded starting from square terms and yielding a Gaussian form that weights nearby paths according to their distance from the MPP [S1]. This approach relies on the geometric concentration of path contributions around the MPP [G].
+Surrounding Path Contribution approximates the complete path integral by taking into account only the contribution of "surrounding" paths [S1]. It differs from the Null-Scattering Path Integral Framework [Null-Scattering Path Integral Framework](3070-null-scattering-path-integral-framework.md) by explicitly parameterizing nearby paths around a single Most Probable Path (MPP) rather than using null-collision sampling. This approach exploits the fact that the action function has a global minimum at the MPP, allowing the integrand to be expanded starting from square terms and yielding a Gaussian form that weights nearby paths according to their distance from the MPP [S1].
 
